The ELF64 format, in `ca::elf`: the structures and constants of `<elf.h>` without including it,
and two views that read them safely. Everything in ca that reads ELF goes through here: the
vDSO lookup (`syscall::vdso_symbol`), the runtime's self-relocation and TLS setup, the
executable range of a backtrace, and the symbolizer's sections. It is also the base of the
dynamic loader.

The structures have the layout of their `<elf.h>` counterparts, checked field by field when the
library builds (`layout.cpp`), so they overlay mapped memory and file bytes directly:

| ca::elf | elf.h | Constants |
|---|---|---|
| `file_header` | `Elf64_Ehdr` | `identity`, `file_types`, `machines` |
| `program_header` | `Elf64_Phdr` | `segment_types`, `segment_flags` |
| `section_header` | `Elf64_Shdr` | `section_types`, `section_flags`, `section_indices` |
| `symbol` | `Elf64_Sym` | `symbol_types`, `symbol_bindings`, `symbol_visibilities` |
| `dynamic_entry` | `Elf64_Dyn` | `dynamic_tags`, `dynamic_flags`, `dynamic_flags_1` |
| `relocation` | `Elf64_Rela` | `x86_64_relocations` |
| `auxiliary_entry` | `Elf64_auxv_t` | `auxiliary_types` |

Fields keep the ELF order with descriptive names (`_segments_offset` is `e_phoff`, `_address`
is `p_vaddr`); the packed fields have accessors (`symbol::type()`, `relocation::symbol()`). Only
ELF64 little-endian is read; ELF32 and big-endian files are refused.

Two views, owning nothing:

- `elf::file` reads a file as it lies on disk (usually a read-only mapping of it). `from` checks
  the header and that the program and section header tables fit inside the bytes, aligned; then
  `sections()`, `name(section)`, `find(".symtab")`, `linked(section)`, `contents(section)`,
  `string(table, offset)` and `symbols(table)` all stay inside the bytes, so a malformed or
  truncated file gives empty results instead of a fault.
- `elf::image` reads an object mapped for execution: the vDSO, the running program, or a library
  a loader mapped. `at(header)` takes a mapped file header, `of(segments, bias)` program headers
  whose bias is known (`bias_of` gives it from the program-headers segment), and `program()` is
  the running program. It reads the dynamic section once: `string`, `symbols`, `value(tag)`, the
  three relocation tables, and `find(name)`, a lookup through the GNU hash table (bloom filter
  first) or else the SysV one.

`image::program()` takes the program headers from the auxiliary vector and the bias from their
PT_PHDR segment. A static PIE (how freestanding ca programs are linked with `-static-pie`) has
none; its ELF header then sits right before its program headers, in the load segment at file
offset 0, so `program()` reads that header through the kernel (`process_vm_readv`, which fails
rather than faults), checks it is ELF64 with its program headers right after it, and takes the
bias from that segment. It works the same under libc, in a static program and in a static PIE.
The program's dynamic section is there only when it has one: a static non-PIE program has none,
even linked with `--export-dynamic`, and its image has empty `dynamic()` and `symbols()`. Only
what the program exports (`--export-dynamic`, or what the shared libraries it links reference)
is in its dynamic symbol table. `ca::trace` takes the executable's range from it, and `ca::dl`
binds plugins to what the program exports.

`elf::hashed_name::of(name)` computes both ELF hashes of a symbol name once, at compile time
when the name is a constant, so a lookup through several images hashes the name only once.

The dynamic section's addresses are link-time addresses in the file, but libc's loader rewrites
them in place to where they were mapped, in the objects it loads (the program included). An
`image` takes an address inside the range the load segments were linked at as link-time and adds
the bias, takes one inside the range they are mapped at as already rebased, and treats any other
as absent. The vDSO, a static program and anything ca maps keep link-time addresses.

```c++
#include "ca/elf.hpp"
#include "ca/syscall.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	const ca::u64 base = ca::syscall::auxiliary(ca::elf::auxiliary_types::vdso).value_or(0U);
	if (base == 0U)
		return 0;
	const auto vdso = ca::elf::image::at(*reinterpret_cast<const ca::elf::file_header*>(base));
	if (!vdso)
		return 1;
	constexpr auto name = ca::elf::hashed_name::of("__vdso_clock_gettime");
	const auto symbol = vdso->find(name);
	if (!symbol || symbol->type() != ca::elf::symbol_types::function)
		return 2;
	using clock_gettime = int (*)(int, void*);
	const auto call = reinterpret_cast<clock_gettime>(vdso->address(symbol->_value));
	long time[2] = {};
	return call(1, time) == 0 && time[0] > 0 ? 0 : 3;
}
```
